        ('ed_stay', 'metric', 'ED stays', 'Emergency department stays. One row per attendance, spanning presentation at the emergency department to physical departure from it.', 'Visits whose first recorded segment is an emergency department segment, counted on the presentation date', null, 'tamanu', 'AIHW', '472757', 'Follows the AIHW METeOR object class "Emergency department stay" (METeOR 472757): the period between when a patient presents at an emergency department and when that person is recorded as having physically departed it. Bounded by "Emergency department stay - presentation date/time" (METeOR 746091, 746096) and "Emergency department stay - physical departure date/time" (METeOR 746076, 746081). Same population as ed_visit - the distinction is framing, not inclusion: ed_visit measures total length of stay to hospital discharge, ed_stay measures the emergency department portion. AIHW registers no length-of-stay data element, so the emitted duration is a BES composition over the AIHW concept. Divergence: Tamanu records the administrative transition out of the emergency department phase, which for an admitted patient can precede physical departure from the department. Pending alignment with the deploying country''s national HMIS definition.', 'count', 'visit', 'facility_id,sex,triage_score,discharge_disposition', null, 'bes-maui', 'approved', 'specs/dbt-model/metric__emergency_stay.md'),
        ('ed_visit', 'metric', 'ED visits', 'Emergency room visits, from arrival in the emergency department to discharge from hospital. One row per visit.', 'Visits whose first recorded segment is an emergency department segment, counted on the presentation date', null, 'tamanu', 'AIHW', '746091', 'Visit concept follows AIHW METeOR "Emergency department stay", counted by presentation date (METeOR 746091). AIHW does publish aggregate indicators in METeOR, but its emergency department indicators are Australian policy performance measures (e.g. National Healthcare Agreement PI 19, METeOR 740847, which requires triage category 4-5 and non-ambulance arrival) rather than a plain attendance count - so the aggregation here is a BES composition over the AIHW concept, not an implementation of an AIHW indicator. Pending alignment with the deploying country''s national HMIS definition.', 'count', 'visit', 'facility_id,sex,triage_score,ed_start__hour,is_admitted', null, 'bes-maui', 'approved', 'specs/dbt-model/metric__emergency_visit.md'),
        ('encounter_diagnosis', 'metric', 'Diagnoses', 'Diagnoses recorded against a clinical encounter. One row per diagnosis, so an encounter with several diagnoses contributes one each.', 'Encounter diagnoses still standing -- excluding those a clinician marked disproven or entered in error -- counted on the date the diagnosis was recorded', null, 'tamanu', 'BES', null, 'A count of recorded diagnoses, which no external body registers as an indicator: ICD-10 is a classification of diagnoses, not a definition of a morbidity count, and WHO''s and AIHW''s morbidity reporting is built on cause-of-admission and cause-of-death groupings over a national coding standard rather than on a plain count of what clinicians recorded. Deployments also differ in what they code diagnoses with -- some use real ICD-10, others a local reference-data list -- so this metric emits the diagnosis as recorded and leaves any grouping to the consumer, which is what lets it serve deployments on either. Pending alignment with the deploying country''s national HMIS definition.', 'count', 'diagnosis', 'facility_id,encounter_type,sex,diagnosis_code,diagnosis,diagnosis_certainty,is_primary', null, 'bes-maui', 'approved', 'specs/dbt-model/metric__encounter_diagnosis.md'),
